{
 "id": "DW-05",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-dw-05-f091fab1d7",
 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 6,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Is it normal for my dishwasher to leave everything wet? Plastic tubs are soaking every time and there's drips on the glasses. It's a Bosch Serie 4."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "No, it is not normal for a Bosch Serie 4 dishwasher to leave plastic tubs soaking and glasses dripping. This indicates a fault that requires attention rather than being standard operation. From what you have described, the most likely cause is rinse aid level / dispenser setting. Are the dishes HOT at the end but wet (a drying issue), or does the whole wash stay COLD (a heating issue) - and is there rinse aid in the dispenser?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The water's definitely hot during the cycle and it's all steamy inside at the end, so it's not a heating problem. The rinse aid symbol is lit on the display and I think it's empty - I use all-in-one tablets though."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the rinse aid. Next: Top up rinse aid and raise the dose. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok, I'll top it up with rinse aid. How do I raise the dose on the Serie 4?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "You can adjust the rinse aid dosage by turning the dial located inside the door near the dispenser. Rotate it clockwise to increase the setting, which is typically numbered from 1 to 6 or 7. From what you have described, the most likely cause is rinse aid level / dispenser setting. Next: Use a full programme with a hot final rinse rather than eco/quick. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Right, I'll top it up and turn the dial up. I've been using Eco 50 every time, so I can try a different programme. Anything else I should change?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "You do not need to change anything in the machine itself. The issue is likely caused by using all-in-one tablets with the Eco 50 programme, as these often lack sufficient salt and rinse aid for effective drying on that setting. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the rinse aid. Now run a normal programme again. Are the dishes coming out dry now, or still wet?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't run it yet, I'll do it after topping up the rinse aid. Should I open the door straight away at the end like I normally do, or does that matter?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "It is generally better to leave the door slightly ajar for a few hours after the cycle finishes to allow steam and moisture to escape. This helps prevent condensation from dripping back onto your dishes and improves drying performance. Next: Let condensation / auto-door drying work (don't seal the door shut immediately). Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok, I'll leave the door ajar after the cycle then. Is there anything else I should check or change, or shall I just run it and see?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "You have already taken the correct steps by topping up the rinse aid and leaving the door ajar to dry. Since you are using Eco 50 programme with hot water and all-in-one tablets, there is no further immediate action required other than observing if the drying improves after a few cycles. From what you have described, the most likely cause is rinse aid level / dispenser setting. Next: Expect plastics to stay wetter than crockery. Let me know what you find."
  }
 ],
